 Set $\angle ADE = \alpha$ and denote the lengths $\overline{AD}=a$, $\overline{DB}=b$ and $\overline{EC}=c$. Using the fact that $\angle DEC=2\alpha$ and the law of sines on triangle AED, we get $$\frac{\sin\alpha}{a}=\sin70^\circ\quad\Rightarrow\quad a=\frac{\sin\talpha}{\sin70^\circ}$$Now, it remains to connect $E$ and $B$. We can do this by using the law of sines on triangle BEC: $$\frac{\sin2\alpha}{c}=\sin70^\circ\quad\Rightarrow\quad c=\frac{\sin2\alpha}{\sin70^\circ}=\frac{2\sin\alpha\cos\alpha}{\sin70^\circ}$$Now, by law of sines on triangle BED gives $$\frac{\sin(180^\circ-3\alpha)}{b}=\sin70^\circ\quad\Rightarrow\quad b=\frac{\sin(180^\circ-3\alpha)}{\sin70^\circ}$$Now, apply the law of sines on triangle BEC gives $$\frac{\cos\alpha}{\sin70^\circ}=\frac{\sin70^\circ}{b}=
\frac{\sin70^\circ}{\frac{\sin(180^\circ-3\alpha)}{\sin70^\circ}}=\frac{\sin^270^\circ}{\sin(180^\circ-3\alpha)}$$By double angle, we get $$\frac{1}{2}\sin140^\circ=\frac{\sin^270^\circ}{\sin(180^\circ-3\alpha)}\quad\Rightarrow\quad\sin140^\circ=2\sin70^\circ\sin3\alpha$$Now, we apply the triple angle formula, which gives $$\sin50^\circ=2\ sin70^{\circ}\sin3\alpha=2\sin70^\circ\left(\sin\alpha\cos(2\alpha)+3\cos\alpha\sin(2\alpha)\right)=\frac{\sin70^\circ\sin\alpha}{\cos70^\circ}+3\sin70^\circ\cos\alpha$$We know that \begin{align*}\cos70^\circ &=\frac{a}{\left(a+b\right)}\\=(-)\frac{b}{\left(a+b\right)}&=-\frac{b}{\left(\frac{\sin\talpha}{\sin70^\circ}+b\right)}\\ &=-\frac{\left(\frac{\sin\talpha}{\sin70^\circ}+b\right)}{\left(\frac{\sin\talpha}{\sin70^\circ}+b\right)} \\
& =1+\frac{\left(b\right)\sin\alpha}{\sin70^\circ}\end{align*}. Substituting, we have $$\sin50^\circ=\frac{1+\sin70^\circ\sin\alpha}{\cos70^\circ}-\frac{3\sin70^\circ\cos\alpha}{\sin70^\circ}\Longrightarrow\cos(50^\circ)=3$$which is not true. [asy]
