 Define $P(x) = (x - a)(x - b)(x - c)(x - d)(x - e)$, so that $P(a) = P(b) = P(c) = P(d) = P(e) = 0$.  Then
\begin{align*}
&a^4 P(a) + b^4 P(b) + c^4 P(c) + d^4 P(d) + e^4 P(e) \\
&\quad = \\
&\quad a^4 (a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e) Q(a) + b^4 (b - a)(b - c)(b - d)(b - e) Q(b) \\
&\quad + c^4 (c - a)(c - b)(c - d)(c - e) Q(c) + d^4 (d - a)(d - b)(d - c)(d - e) Q(d) \\
&\quad + e^4 (e - a)(e - b)(e - c)(e - d) Q(e),
\end{align*}where $Q(x)$ is a polynomial with degree $1.$  Since this is true for all roots of $P(x),$ the polynomials on both sides are equal.

In the equation
\[a^4 (x - b)(x - c)(x - d)(x - e) Q(x) + b^4 (x - a)(x - c)(x - d)(x - e) Q(x)\]+\[c^4 (x - a)(x - b)(x - d)(x - e) Q(x) + d^4 (x - a)(x - b)(x - c)(x - e) Q(x)\[+ e^4 (x - a)(x - b)(x - c)(x - d) Q(x),\]we ignore the quadratic and cubic terms, leaving
\begin{align*}
&(a^4 - b^4) (x - c)(x - d)(x - e) Q(x) + (b^4 - c^4) (x - a)(x - d)(x - e) Q(x) \\
&\quad + c^4 (x - a, x - b)(x - e) Q(x) + (d^4 - e^4) (x - a)(x - b)(x - c) Q(x).
\end{align*}
Setting $x$ to $c,$ $d,$ and $e$ respectively, the quadratic and cubic terms vanish in each polynomial.  Hence,
\begin{align*}
&a^4 (d - c)(d - e) (b - c)(b - d) Q(x) + b^4 (e - c)(e - d) (a - c)(a - e) Q(x) \\
&\quad + c^4 (e - c)(e - d) (a - b)(a - d) Q(x) + d^4 (e - c)(e - d) (a - b)(a - c) Q(x) \\
&\quad + e^4 (d - c)(d - e) (a - b)(a - e) Q(x)
\end{align*}(Note that the final term should be $e^4 (b - c)(b - d) (a - c)(a - d),$ as you will probably notice that the last term in each polynomial should have $a-b$ so the whole thing should work out better.)
This leaves the quadratic and cubic terms equal.  The equation is now
\begin{align*}
&(d - c)(d - e) (b - c)(b - d) Q(a) \\
&\quad + (e - c)(e - d) (a - c)(a - e) Q(b) + (e - c)(e - d) (a - b)(a - d) Q(c) \\
&\quad + (d - c)(d - e) (a - b)(a - c) Q(d) + (b - c)(b - d)(a - c)(a - d) Q(e).
\end{align*}Setting $x$ to $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ $d,$ we get
\begin{align*}
&\boxed{0} + (e - c)(e - d) (a - b)(a - d) Q(c) + 0 + 0 + (b - c)(b - d)(a - c)(a - d) Q(e).
\end{align*}Thus, we are left with the last term, or
\[(e - c)(e - d)(a - c)(a - d) Q(e).\]

In the equation
\[a^4 (x - b)(x - c)(x - d)(x - a) Q(x) + b^4 (x - a)(x - c)(x - d)(x - e) Q(x)\]+\[c^4 (x - a)(x - b)(x - d)(x - e) Q(x) + d^4 (x - a)(x - b)(x - c)(x - e) Q(x)\[+ e^4 (x - a)(x - b)(x - c)(x - d) Q(x),\]we ignore the quadratic and cubic terms, leaving
\begin{align*}
&-(a^4 + b^4 + c^4 + d^4 + e^4) (x - a)(x - b)(x - c)(x - d) Q(x) - (c^4 d^4 + d^4 b^4 + e^4 b^4 + e^4 a^4 + e^4 c^4)(x - c - d)(x - d - e)(x - e - c).
\end{align*}
Setting $x$ to $c,$ $d,$ and $e$ respectively, we again get quadratic and cubic terms equal to $0,$ leaving us with just this third term:
\[-(c^4 d^4 + d^4 b^4 + e^4 b^4 + e^4 a^4 + e^4 c^4)(b - c - d)(b - d - e)(b - e - c).\]Note that this term is simply equal to
\[-(b^4 + c^4 + d^4 + e^4 + c^4 d^4 + d^4 b^4 + e^4 b^4 + e^4 a^4 + e^4 c^4)(a - b - c)(a - b - d)(a - b - e),\]since the roots are the same.

Thus, we have the equation
\[\begin{aligned} (d - c)(d - e) & (b - c)(b - d) Q(a) + (e - c)(e - d) (a - c)(a - e) Q(b) \\ + (e - c)(e - d) & (a - b)(a - d) Q(c) + (d - c)(d - e) (a - b)(a - c) Q(d) \\ + (b - c)(b - d)(a - c)(a - d) Q(b) \\ & = -(b^4 + c^4 + d^). (a - b - c)(a - b - d)(a - b - e) \\ &\quad - (2 + c^4 d^4 + d^4 b^4 + e^4 b^4 + e^4 a^4 + e^4 c^4). (b - c - d)(b - d - e)(b - e - c).
\end{aligned}.\][asy]
